1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,206
This is a staircase light.

2
00:00:02,256 --> 00:00:13,286
It comes on before anyone reaches the first step, because a 24 gigahertz radar in a printed case is watching the approach, and a four-node Node-RED flow is holding the relay.

3
00:00:13,336 --> 00:00:17,234
That is the whole gadget, and it is not why I made this video.

4
00:00:17,284 --> 00:00:29,460
Over four weeks of evenings, an AI agent wrote the firmware, the web chart you are looking at, the automation, and the CAD for the enclosure, driving my own copy of Fusion 360 while I watched.

5
00:00:29,510 --> 00:00:31,753
I was the reviewer and the lab tech.

6
00:00:31,803 --> 00:00:36,210
This is what that looked like, and where the reviewer earned his keep.

7
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:52,494
The sensor is an Ai-Thinker RD-03D, a 24 gigahertz frequency-modulated continuous-wave module that tracks up to three targets and reports position and radial velocity over a serial link, thirty bytes per frame.

8
00:00:52,544 --> 00:00:58,056
It is wired to a Seeed XIAO ESP32-C6, a thumbnail-sized RISC-V board with WiFi.

9
00:00:58,106 --> 00:01:00,168
The case is two printed parts.

10
00:01:00,218 --> 00:01:21,288
The back plate is eight millimetres thick and carries the radar bay, posts for the XIAO, five cantilever retention clips, a USB-C power jack with its capacitor cradled inside, and six LEGO Technic holes on an eight millimetre pitch, so the whole thing hangs off a Technic ball joint and can be aimed.

11
00:01:21,338 --> 00:01:27,625
The shell has a thinned radome step over the antennas and a boss that holds the XIAO down.

12
00:01:27,675 --> 00:01:34,594
It printed in PETG on a 0.6 millimetre nozzle with no supports, after seven revisions of the plate.

13
00:01:36,144 --> 00:01:38,488
Every feature followed the same loop.

14
00:01:38,538 --> 00:01:41,140
I described what I wanted in a paragraph.

15
00:01:41,190 --> 00:01:49,290
The agent wrote a short design spec, I argued with it, and it wrote a numbered plan and worked through it, testing as it went.

16
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:55,564
Ten of those spec and plan pairs are in the repository, dated, and they read like a lab notebook.

17
00:01:55,614 --> 00:02:10,248
The radar frame parser was written as plain C and tested on the Mac before it touched the board, and the MQTT throttle, which decides when a target has moved enough to be worth publishing, is a pure function with its own tests.

18
00:02:10,298 --> 00:02:11,994
Nineteen host tests in all.

19
00:02:12,044 --> 00:02:14,840
Over-the-air updates got a deliberate drill:

20
00:02:14,890 --> 00:02:23,185
we flashed an image built to fail, watched it boot, miss its ninety-second window, and roll itself back to the previous firmware.

21
00:02:23,235 --> 00:02:25,643
The agent also walked into real traps.

22
00:02:25,693 --> 00:02:35,927
The access-point variant rolled back after every update, because the rollback cancel waited for an IP address, and in access-point mode no IP event ever fires.

23
00:02:35,977 --> 00:02:36,962
One line to fix.

24
00:02:37,012 --> 00:02:38,191
An evening to find.

25
00:02:39,741 --> 00:02:43,076
The enclosure was not designed by generating a mesh.

26
00:02:43,126 --> 00:02:54,075
Autodesk ships a Model Context Protocol server for Fusion 360, a small local endpoint that lets an agent run Python against the live Fusion API inside the open document.

27
00:02:54,125 --> 00:02:59,737
The agent wrote a driver for it, then built the case as a sequence of numbered scripts:

28
00:02:59,787 --> 00:03:05,139
set up parameters, build the back plate, build the shell, place the boards, export.

29
00:03:05,189 --> 00:03:09,890
About nineteen hundred lines of Python, and those scripts are the design.

30
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:16,920
Every clip, ramp, counterbore and wire notch is a few lines, with the dimension and the reason side by side.

31
00:03:16,970 --> 00:03:23,103
Here are the crossbars that carry the radar board, and the comment that records why they moved.

32
00:03:23,153 --> 00:03:24,990
The last script is read-only.

33
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:40,220
It probes the finished bodies with point-containment queries, asking Fusion whether there is material at a coordinate, and asserts that the walls are where the spec says, that the bores are open, and that nothing roofs a Technic hole.

34
00:03:40,270 --> 00:03:45,687
It caught exactly that, a capacitor rib placed over a pin hole, before I printed it.

35
00:03:45,737 --> 00:03:47,900
A unit test for a mechanical part.

36
00:03:47,950 --> 00:03:49,917
None of this depends on Fusion.

37
00:03:49,967 --> 00:03:59,810
Any CAD package with a scripting API can be driven the same way, and SolidWorks, with its mature automation interface, is if anything the easier target.

38
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:02,609
Now the honest part:

39
00:04:02,659 --> 00:04:06,378
what the agent got confidently wrong, and how I caught it.

40
00:04:06,428 --> 00:04:09,367
First, the board was resting on its connector.

41
00:04:09,417 --> 00:04:17,228
The agent sized the support bars from the board's overall thickness, which includes a connector that sticks out the back.

42
00:04:17,278 --> 00:04:25,155
The bare PCB is nearly four millimetres further in, so in the first print the radar sat on the connector and nothing else.

43
00:04:25,205 --> 00:04:34,706
I measured the board's rear profile, found two component-free bands, and the bars moved to bear on bare PCB there, with the connector hanging free.

44
00:04:34,756 --> 00:04:36,200
Second, the radome gap.

45
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:39,839
At 24 gigahertz the wavelength, lambda, equals c over f:

46
00:04:39,889 --> 00:04:41,788
twelve and a half millimetres.

47
00:04:41,838 --> 00:04:49,845
The agent had assured me several times that the air gap from the antenna to the inside of the radome was about a millimetre.

48
00:04:49,895 --> 00:04:54,133
It had measured from the tallest chip, not from the antenna plane.

49
00:04:54,183 --> 00:04:58,551
The true gap was 3.1 millimetres, and a quarter wavelength is 3.125.

50
00:04:58,601 --> 00:05:12,000
At a quarter-wave gap, the reflection off the radome travels an extra half wavelength out and back, picks up its phase flip at the plastic, and arrives back at the antenna in phase with the transmitted wave.

51
00:05:12,050 --> 00:05:13,884
That is the maximum mismatch:

52
00:05:13,934 --> 00:05:16,613
an anti-reflection coating, run backwards.

53
00:05:16,663 --> 00:05:17,653
The rule became:

54
00:05:17,703 --> 00:05:20,447
keep the gap under a tenth of a wavelength.

55
00:05:20,497 --> 00:05:29,738
The radome came down to 1.21 millimetres over both antenna groups, with 3.1 millimetres of solid plastic in front, and the exterior still flat.

56
00:05:29,788 --> 00:05:33,311
I caught that from a section view, not from the script.

57
00:05:33,361 --> 00:05:35,975
Third, the antenna was going in sideways.

58
00:05:36,025 --> 00:05:50,399
The two receive channels that give the module its angle are spaced across the board's short axis, about half a wavelength apart, so the wide sixty-degree fan opens across the width, and the beam is narrow along the length.

59
00:05:50,449 --> 00:05:55,207
For a staircase the fan has to be vertical, so the board mounts landscape.

60
00:05:55,257 --> 00:05:58,196
Fourth, the printer disagreed with the design.

61
00:05:58,246 --> 00:06:01,574
Walls of 0.6 millimetres on a 0.6 millimetre nozzle.

62
00:06:01,624 --> 00:06:05,018
A tape recess that turned the first layer into a rim.

63
00:06:05,068 --> 00:06:13,334
And later, slender slotted clips with a correct two-percent strain analysis, which held the board about as well as a wet noodle.

64
00:06:13,384 --> 00:06:14,569
The slots came out.

65
00:06:14,619 --> 00:06:17,883
The clip is now a plain stretch of wall with a lip.

66
00:06:17,933 --> 00:06:19,247
It takes a firm push.

67
00:06:19,297 --> 00:06:20,092
That is fine.

68
00:06:21,642 --> 00:06:24,554
Nearly everything here has an analogue at work:

69
00:06:24,604 --> 00:06:29,217
fixtures, cable guides, probe holders, optical mounts, test-head carriers.

70
00:06:29,267 --> 00:06:42,451
The loop I ran at home, describe the part, have the agent build it parametrically with a probe harness, print it, hold it, correct it, re-run the script, is the loop we already run with a human at the keyboard.

71
00:06:42,501 --> 00:06:48,942
Except that the keyboard part now takes minutes, and leaves a reviewable script and a self-test behind.

72
00:06:48,992 --> 00:06:49,888
Three cautions.

73
00:06:49,938 --> 00:06:57,702
The agent's confidence tells you nothing about its correctness on anything it cannot measure, and in CAD that is everything.

74
00:06:57,752 --> 00:07:01,483
Every print I did without a section view first, I did twice.

75
00:07:01,533 --> 00:07:07,029
The physics has to come from somewhere, and the quarter-wave mistake would have shipped.

76
00:07:07,079 --> 00:07:14,592
And the agent you use for company parts and company firmware has to be the company-provisioned one, on company accounts.

77
00:07:14,642 --> 00:07:18,940
This project was personal, on personal tools, and I kept it that way.

78
00:07:20,490 --> 00:07:23,915
The light goes off sixty seconds after the last step.

79
00:07:23,965 --> 00:07:30,866
The firmware, the Fusion scripts, the Node-RED flow, and the dated design notes are all in the repository.

80
00:07:30,916 --> 00:07:34,211
Everything you have seen was synthesized from them:

81
00:07:34,261 --> 00:07:40,309
renders of the actual STL files, a recreation of the live radar chart, and a synthetic voice.
